Subject: How is LOD calculated?
Message-ID: <tg6XS09N39c.nvJlBQjEBwAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/tg6XS09N39c/m/nvJlBQjEBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Is this merely a function of recombination frequency? How is it possible to have both a high RF and a high LOD? I know in the genetic mapping tutorial, switched alleles are proposed as the answer, but how does this work out mathematically?

Best wishes
Alex

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Sun Apr  7 13:24:20 2019
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Sun, 07 Apr 2019 13:24:20 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] How is LOD calculated?
Message-ID: <tg6XS09N39c.OPgjVMPIBwAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <tg6XS09N39c.nvJlBQjEBwAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <tg6XS09N39c.nvJlBQjEBwAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/tg6XS09N39c/m/OPgjVMPIBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It’s the log_10 likelihood ratio for testing H0: rf = 1/2 vs the alternative rf /= 1/2.
So it becomes large when the estimated rf is away from 1/2, either large or small.

karl

